{#
    One series as horizontal bars: `rows` is a list of {label, value}, largest
    first, each with an optional `detail` (a share) after the value. The form
    for many named categories (forty-odd countries), where columns would leave
    no room for their labels on a phone.
    `summary` is the caller's line under the title, as on ColumnChart.

    Plain HTML and CSS, no chart library and no JavaScript (ADR 0048). Every
    label and value is visible text, so only the bars are hidden from screen
    readers. The label column is min-w-0 and truncates, so a long name never
    widens the chart past its container.
#}
{% props title, summary, rows %}
{% set peak = max(1, max(rows|map(r => r.value))) %}
<figure {{ attributes.defaults({class: 'rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 shadow-sm'}) }}>
    <figcaption class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-3">
        <span class="font-medium text-slate-800">{{ title }}</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-slate-500">{{ summary }}</span>
    </figcaption>
    <ol class="space-y-[2px] text-sm">
        {% for r in rows %}
            <li class="grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,8rem)_1fr_auto] items-center gap-2 sm:grid-cols-[minmax(0,12rem)_1fr_auto]">
                <span class="truncate text-slate-700" title="{{ r.label }}">{{ r.label }}</span>
                <span class="h-4" aria-hidden="true">
                    <span class="block h-full rounded-r bg-brand-600" style="width: {{ max(1, r.value / peak * 100)|round(1) }}%"></span>
                </span>
                {# Fixed widths, not the content's: each row is its own grid, and
                   bars only compare if every row's bar track is equally wide. #}
                <span class="tabular-nums"><span class="inline-block w-8 text-right text-slate-700">{{ r.value|number_format }}</span>{% if r.detail is defined %} <span class="inline-block w-14 text-right text-slate-500">{{ r.detail }}</span>{% endif %}</span>
            </li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ol>
</figure>
